WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.566 --> 00:00:05.506
What is RabbitMQ, and what makes it one
of the most popular message brokers out there?

00:00:06.356 --> 00:00:07.536
My name is Whitney Lee.

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:09.606
I'm a Cloud Developer here at IBM.

00:00:10.206 --> 00:00:14.476
Before I dig in, please, don't
forget to hit that subscribe button.

00:00:15.986 --> 00:00:19.286
So, let's travel back in time to
the days of monolithic architecture.

00:00:19.896 --> 00:00:22.716
Back then, application components
were tightly coupled.

00:00:23.246 --> 00:00:24.906
That means they were directly connected.

00:00:27.116 --> 00:00:34.466
So, in a simple retail application, if we had
a checkout service and it needed to communicate

00:00:34.466 --> 00:00:38.016
with an inventory service, that
would be done directly through,

00:00:38.046 --> 00:00:40.246
usually through a TCP connection.

00:00:43.556 --> 00:00:45.456
So, this had some limitations.

00:00:45.976 --> 00:00:48.006
As soon as the checkout sent the message,

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:51.316
it would need to hear a reply before
it could move on to the next task.

00:00:51.696 --> 00:00:56.696
Or worse, if the inventory service went
down, it would try over and over again

00:00:56.696 --> 00:00:58.476
until it was able to make that connection.

00:00:59.066 --> 00:01:04.436
Or, if a lot of checkouts happened at once the
inventory service wouldn't be able to keep up

00:01:04.436 --> 00:01:06.206
and the whole system would get bogged down.

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:10.656
So, that's why message queues were
created -- or, message brokers --

00:01:11.096 --> 00:01:15.246
and those will sit, a message queue sits
in between the two services that need

00:01:15.246 --> 00:01:16.616
to communication with one another.

00:01:18.106 --> 00:01:22.536
So, with a message queue, a checkout
can add a message to the queue

00:01:22.836 --> 00:01:26.056
and then immediately move on to the next task.

00:01:26.106 --> 00:01:31.396
And then similarly, the inventory, when
it's ready, can consume from the queue,

00:01:31.556 --> 00:01:35.036
process the message and then
immediately consume the next message.

00:01:35.106 --> 00:01:39.176
So, this is going to decouple
the two applications.

00:01:42.736 --> 00:01:45.696
A message broker is also going
to help with scalability.

00:01:46.296 --> 00:01:51.026
So, the inventory...if a lot of checkouts
happen at once, the queue begins to fill,

00:01:51.436 --> 00:01:57.026
you can have more than one consuming service
-- more than one inventory, in our case --

00:01:58.986 --> 00:02:03.856
to read from the queue to handle the amount
of workload that the checkout is producing,

00:02:03.856 --> 00:02:06.316
and that's going to make
the system more scalable.

00:02:09.886 --> 00:02:14.856
Another big benefit of message queues is that
the queue itself can sit on its own machine.

00:02:17.196 --> 00:02:23.096
So, in that case, it can offload some of
the work that's done by the Web application

00:02:23.096 --> 00:02:25.066
and make the whole system more performant.

00:02:29.856 --> 00:02:32.596
So, let's talk about RabbitMQ.

00:02:33.216 --> 00:02:38.516
RabbitMQ is an implementation
of the AMQP message model --

00:02:38.916 --> 00:02:44.466
that's Advanced Message Queueing
Protocol -- and specifically, Version 091.

00:02:46.236 --> 00:02:51.886
So, with this type of message model,
the producer, in our case the checkout,

00:02:51.886 --> 00:03:00.446
the service that produces the messages, instead
of producing directly to a message queue,

00:03:00.736 --> 00:03:02.976
it's going to produce to an exchange.

00:03:03.126 --> 00:03:07.676
So, you can think of an exchange
as a post office.

00:03:08.046 --> 00:03:09.596
It's going to receive all the messages

00:03:09.596 --> 00:03:12.966
and then distribute them
according to how they're addressed.

00:03:13.346 --> 00:03:19.586
An exchange could be connected to many
queues; in our case, we're going to do two.

00:03:23.866 --> 00:03:30.076
And then, the queues are connected to
the consuming services or our consumers.

00:03:30.686 --> 00:03:32.826
So, we'll have one called inventory

00:03:32.826 --> 00:03:41.106
and then we'll do one called shipping
might need to consume from a checkout.

00:03:45.706 --> 00:03:48.866
So, the checkout will send
a message to the exchange.

00:03:49.586 --> 00:03:54.096
The exchange is connected to queues
through connections called bindings,

00:03:54.416 --> 00:03:57.706
and these bindings can be
referenced by the binding key.

00:04:02.076 --> 00:04:04.776
And then our consuming applications --

00:04:04.776 --> 00:04:12.706
or, consumers, consuming services
-- those subscribe to the queues.

00:04:12.706 --> 00:04:19.196
So, AMQP, RabbitMQ this is the message
broker here, this part of the system.

00:04:24.306 --> 00:04:30.066
One thing that's great about this
message model I the flexibility

00:04:30.146 --> 00:04:35.346
with which the messages can move through the
system, and that flexibility is largely in part

00:04:35.396 --> 00:04:37.576
to the different types of exchanges available.

00:04:37.636 --> 00:04:43.176
So, the first type of exchange that
the system can do is a fanout exchange.

00:04:45.206 --> 00:04:48.236
With a fanout exchange, checkout
will produce to the exchange,

00:04:48.236 --> 00:04:52.396
the exchange will duplicate the message and send
it to every single queue that it knows about.

00:04:53.586 --> 00:04:55.236
Or, we have a direct exchange.

00:04:58.046 --> 00:05:01.776
With the direct exchange, the checkout
will produce the message and then

00:05:01.776 --> 00:05:04.346
that message will get a routing key.

00:05:07.826 --> 00:05:12.816
So, with a direct exchange the routing
key is being compared to the binding key;

00:05:12.816 --> 00:05:16.966
and if it's an exact match, then the message
will move through the system accordingly.

00:05:18.346 --> 00:05:20.396
Next, we have a topic exchange.

00:05:20.556 --> 00:05:26.506
With a topic exchange, we can do a partial match
between the routing key and the binding key.

00:05:26.506 --> 00:05:30.356
So, if we had a routing key on
this message called ship.shoes

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:40.966
and the binding key was called ship.any
and the exchange type was a topic exchange,

00:05:40.966 --> 00:05:43.806
that message would get routed
through to that, this queue.

00:05:45.676 --> 00:05:47.776
There's also a header exchange.

00:05:50.566 --> 00:05:55.096
With a header exchange, the routing key is
ignored completely, and the message is moved

00:05:55.096 --> 00:05:56.696
through the system according to the header.

00:05:56.866 --> 00:06:00.966
And then finally, we have a default exchange.

00:06:01.306 --> 00:06:03.816
This exchange is unique only to RabbitMQ.

00:06:03.816 --> 00:06:06.486
It's not part of the AMQP message model.

00:06:07.096 --> 00:06:10.066
So, the default exchange is
also called a nameless exchange.

00:06:12.126 --> 00:06:14.866
And with the default exchange, the routing key,

00:06:15.076 --> 00:06:17.956
let's say the routing key
of this message is inv.

00:06:18.376 --> 00:06:21.656
The routing key is getting tied
to the name of the queue itself.

00:06:21.656 --> 00:06:27.896
So, if this queue is named inv, then the
message would route through to there.

00:06:27.896 --> 00:06:33.686
So, there are a couple of main benefits of
RabbitMQ right now with the architecture,

00:06:33.686 --> 00:06:38.846
and one is a tremendous amount of flexibility
you have moving messages through your system.

00:06:39.126 --> 00:06:43.236
In fact, they say you can design your system
with whatever you want, the sky's the limit,

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:47.776
and then later configure it RabbitMQ to
work with your system as opposed to needing

00:06:47.776 --> 00:06:51.366
to know RabbitMQ's limitations and
designing your system accordingly.

00:06:52.336 --> 00:06:56.856
Also, with other message brokers the
broker administrator when they set

00:06:56.856 --> 00:07:01.756
up the message model, that's when all
the configuration for how message moves

00:07:01.756 --> 00:07:03.966
through the system, that's
when it's all defined.

00:07:04.366 --> 00:07:08.386
But with RabbitMQ, the way the message moves

00:07:08.386 --> 00:07:12.236
through the system is largely
a part of the message metadata.

00:07:12.856 --> 00:07:18.206
So, in this case, it's the application and
the developer that has a lot of control

00:07:18.206 --> 00:07:22.156
with the way messages move through the
system rather than the broker administrator.

00:07:23.676 --> 00:07:28.286
Another great benefit to RabbitMQ
is that it is cloud friendly.

00:07:28.376 --> 00:07:32.576
It is easy to get started.

00:07:33.816 --> 00:07:39.726
You can deploy an instance of it on
Docker or other containerization software.

00:07:40.166 --> 00:07:43.706
It also can run as a cluster, so
that means it's fault tolerant,

00:07:43.836 --> 00:07:46.366
highly available and has high throughput.

00:07:48.276 --> 00:07:51.146
RabbitMQ can do cross language communication.

00:07:54.046 --> 00:07:59.526
So, if a message is produced by a checkout
and go, it can be consumed by inventory

00:07:59.526 --> 00:08:03.046
in JavaScript and consumed
by shipping in Python.

00:08:03.336 --> 00:08:05.416
And really the possibilities are endless.

00:08:05.416 --> 00:08:06.996
It has a very extensive library.

00:08:07.226 --> 00:08:09.816
It has good security.

00:08:12.416 --> 00:08:18.386
It supports FASL, LDAP and TLS for
authentication and authorization.

00:08:19.216 --> 00:08:20.736
It does message acknowledgements.

00:08:23.216 --> 00:08:27.566
So, message acknowledgements, when a message
is in a queue and it goes to the consumer,

00:08:28.086 --> 00:08:32.466
the message stays in the queue until
the consumer lets the broker know

00:08:32.706 --> 00:08:34.316
that it has received the message.

00:08:34.616 --> 00:08:36.766
And only then is the message
taken out of the queue,

00:08:37.106 --> 00:08:39.536
and that prevents the system
from losing any messages.

00:08:42.136 --> 00:08:43.916
It has great management.

00:08:47.096 --> 00:08:51.266
So, right out of the box you have a
really good browser based management UI

00:08:51.266 --> 00:08:54.416
as well as incentive CLI tools.

00:08:55.036 --> 00:08:58.516
Not only that, but the open source
community has created plugins

00:08:58.516 --> 00:09:02.686
that really enrich the management
and moderning part of RabbitMQ.

00:09:02.686 --> 00:09:09.236
And speaking of that open source
community, it has created a lot of plugins

00:09:09.926 --> 00:09:12.966
that can enrich most aspects of RabbitMQ.

00:09:12.966 --> 00:09:16.776
There are many tools created,
there are lots of clients,

00:09:16.776 --> 00:09:21.956
it's so evolved that RabbitMQ now
supports other message models.

00:09:21.986 --> 00:09:29.276
So, not just AMQP 091 but you can do
MQTT, Stomp, AMQP 1.0 for example.

00:09:30.936 --> 00:09:33.126
So, this has been an overview of RabbitMQ.

00:09:33.126 --> 00:09:37.926
The big takeaways for you are the
flexibility with the ways the messages move

00:09:37.926 --> 00:09:43.496
through the system; the fact that the
message metadata defines how messages move

00:09:43.496 --> 00:09:46.726
through the system as opposed
to the broker administrator;

00:09:46.726 --> 00:09:49.846
and then, it's also super cloud friendly.

00:09:50.316 --> 00:09:51.026
Thank you.

00:09:51.456 --> 00:09:54.206
If you have questions, please
drop us a line below.

